+### Valid Error Handling Patterns for `query-requires-error-alert`
+
+**Note**: If you have a valid error handling component or attribute that is not recognized by this rule, you need to update the corresponding lists in [query-requires-error-alert.ts](./src/rules/query-requires-error-alert.ts):
+- Add new error handling components to the `errorHandlingComponents` Set (around line 38)
+- Add new error handling attributes to the `errorHandlingAttributes` Set (around line 45)
+
+This rule accepts multiple ways to handle errors from queries and mutations:
+
+#### For Queries (useQuery and custom query hooks):
+
+1. **Error Alert Components** - Render error alert components conditionally based on error state:
+   ```tsx
+   // Using destructured isError
+   const { data, isError } = useQuery(...);
+   {isError && <UnexpectedErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Using destructured error property
+   const { data, error } = useQuery(...);
+   {error && <ErrorAlert heading="Error" text={error.message} />}
+   
+   // Using object access (isError)
+   const query = useQuery(...);
+   {query.isError && <ErrorAlert heading="Error" text="Operation failed" />}
+   
+   // Using object access (error)
+   const query = useQuery(...);
+   {query.error && <UnexpectedErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Using rest element pattern
+   const { data, ...queryState } = useQuery(...);
+   {queryState.isError && <ErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Complex conditions supported
+   const { isError: isError1 } = useQuery(...);
+   const { isError: isError2 } = useQuery(...);
+   {(isError1 || isError2) && <UnexpectedErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Derived error variables
+   const hasError = query1.isError || query2.isError;
+   {hasError && <ErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Ternary and if statements
+   {isError ? <ErrorAlert /> : null}
+   if (query.isError) return <ErrorAlert />;
+   ```
+
+2. **Component Props with Error State** - Pass error state to recognized error-handling attributes:
+   ```tsx
+   // Recognized attributes: isInvalid, errorMessage, isError, error
+   const { isError, error } = useQuery(...);
+   <TextInput isInvalid={isError} errorMessage={error?.message} />
+   <Select isError={query.isError} />
+   <Input error={error?.message} />
+   
+   // Complex expressions supported
+   <Input isInvalid={query1.isError || query2.isError} />
+   <TextInput errorMessage={error1?.message ?? error2?.message} />
+   ```
+
+3. **throwOnError Option** - Let error boundaries handle it:
+   ```tsx
+   const { data } = useQuery({ ..., throwOnError: true });
+   ```
+
+**Note**: Queries support both destructured (`{isError}`, `{error}`) and non-destructured (`query.isError`, `query.error`) patterns, as well as complex expressions combining multiple error states.
+
+#### For Mutations (useMutation and custom mutation hooks):
+
+1. **Error Alert Components** - Render error alert components conditionally:
+   ```tsx
+   // Using destructured isError
+   const { mutate, isError } = useMutation(...);
+   {isError && <UnexpectedErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Using destructured error property
+   const { mutate, error } = useMutation(...);
+   {error && <ErrorAlert heading="Error" text={error.message} />}
+   
+   // Using object access (error)
+   const mutation = useMutation(...);
+   {mutation.error && <ErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Using object access (isError)
+   const mutation = useMutation(...);
+   {mutation.isError && <UnexpectedErrorAlert />}
+   
+   // Using rest element pattern
+   const { mutate, ...mutationState } = useMutation(...);
+   {mutationState.error && <ErrorAlert />}
+   ```
+
+2. **Component Props with Error State** - Pass error state to recognized attributes:
+   ```tsx
+   const { mutate, isError, error } = useMutation(...);
+   <TextInput isInvalid={isError} errorMessage={error?.message} />
+   <Button isError={mutation.isError} />
+   ```
+
+3. **onError Callback** - Handle errors in mutation options:
+   ```tsx
+   const mutation = useMutation({
+     mutationFn: updateData,
+     onError: (error) => {
+       toast.error('Update failed');
+     }
+   });
+   
+   // Or in mutate call
+   mutation.mutate(data, {
+     onError: (error) => {
+       console.error(error);
+     }
+   });
+   ```
+
+4. **Catch Chain** - Handle errors via promise catch:
+   ```tsx
+   mutation.mutateAsync(data).catch((error) => {
+     toast.error('Operation failed');
+   });
+   ```
+
+**Note**: Mutations now support the same destructured and object access patterns as queries, in addition to `onError` callbacks and catch chains.
